# Reproducible synthetic training split and held-out evaluation

## Goal

Show the dataset and experiment contract of WLB.03 on the versioned
synthetic room stream: split by time before windowing, derive training
statistics from the training split only, fit a tiny consumer-owned Axon model,
score it on held-out rows against the persistence baseline, and record a
manifest with content digests. No accuracy claim follows from any number
below; the data is synthetic and the separate plain-Nx coefficient remains an
inspectable reference baseline.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:nx, "~> 0.13.1"},
  {:axon, "~> 0.8.1"},
  {:exla, "~> 0.13.1"}
])
```

The requirements above are published-artifact requirements. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`docs/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout. Axon and EXLA are named
here because this notebook is the explicit model/backend integration profile;
they remain optional dependencies of the Lab package.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Observation rows, windows, encoded batches, decoded predictions | `wotex_nx` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Nx.Window`, `Encoder`, `Decoder` |
| Synthetic stream, split, statistics, model, metrics, manifest | consumer (`wotex_lab`) | this notebook and `Wotex.Lab.Simulators.Thermal` |
| Content digests | `wotex_lab` | `Wotex.Lab.Evidence.Digest` |
| Axon model, training loop, parameter artifact | consumer (`wotex_lab`) | `Wotex.Lab.Experiments.RoomModel` |

## Run

```elixir
alias Wotex.DataSchema
alias Wotex.Lab.Evidence.Digest
alias Wotex.Lab.Examples.WindowAnomaly
alias Wotex.Lab.Experiments.RoomModel
alias Wotex.Lab.Simulators.Thermal
alias Wotex.Nx.{Decoder, OutputSchema, Prediction}
```

Generate the stream. The manifest records the equation, seed, step, initial
state, disturbance schedule and units; `source_mode` is `synthetic`.

```elixir
simulation = Thermal.generate(seed: 11, count: 64, heater: %{20 => 3.0, 44 => 2.0})
{:ok, observations} = WindowAnomaly.observations(simulation.samples)
values = Enum.map(simulation.samples, & &1.value)
times = Enum.map(simulation.samples, & &1.observed_at)
{simulation.manifest["equation"], length(values), simulation.manifest["source_mode"]}
```

Split by time first: the first 48 samples train, the last 16 are held out.
Windows are built after the split, so no window crosses the boundary.

```elixir
split_at = 48
{train_values, test_values} = Enum.split(values, split_at)
{train_times, test_times} = Enum.split(times, split_at)
boundary = Enum.at(times, split_at)

windows = fn xs -> Enum.chunk_every(xs, 2, 1, :discard) end
train_windows = windows.(train_values)
test_windows = windows.(test_values)
window_times = fn ts -> Enum.chunk_every(ts, 2, 1, :discard) end

no_leak? =
  Enum.all?(window_times.(train_times), fn [_a, b] -> b < boundary end) and
    Enum.all?(window_times.(test_times), fn [a, _b] -> a >= boundary end)

%{boundary: boundary, train_windows: length(train_windows), test_windows: length(test_windows), no_leak?: no_leak?}
```

Training statistics come from the training split only; the held-out split is
normalized with them, never with its own.

```elixir
train_tensor = Nx.tensor(train_values, type: :f32)
mean = Nx.mean(train_tensor)
std = Nx.standard_deviation(train_tensor)
normalize = fn tensor -> Nx.divide(Nx.subtract(tensor, mean), std) end

test_tensor = Nx.tensor(test_values, type: :f32)
test_mean_seen? = Nx.to_number(mean) == Nx.to_number(Nx.mean(test_tensor))
{Nx.to_number(mean), Nx.to_number(std), test_mean_seen?}
```

A one-parameter autoregressive model `x[n+1] ~ a * x[n]` in normalized
space, fitted in closed form on the training windows with Nx. It remains a
transparent reference next to the trained Axon lane.

```elixir
pairs = fn ws -> {Nx.tensor(Enum.map(ws, &hd/1), type: :f32), Nx.tensor(Enum.map(ws, &List.last/1), type: :f32)} end
{train_x, train_y} = pairs.(train_windows)
{test_x, test_y} = pairs.(test_windows)

{nx_x, nx_y} = {normalize.(train_x), normalize.(train_y)}
coefficient = Nx.divide(Nx.sum(Nx.multiply(nx_x, nx_y)), Nx.sum(Nx.multiply(nx_x, nx_x)))

predict = fn x -> Nx.add(Nx.multiply(Nx.multiply(normalize.(x), coefficient), std), mean) end
mae = fn prediction, truth -> Nx.to_number(Nx.mean(Nx.abs(Nx.subtract(prediction, truth)))) end

model_mae = mae.(predict.(test_x), test_y)
persistence_mae = mae.(test_x, test_y)
%{coefficient: Nx.to_number(coefficient), model_mae: model_mae, persistence_mae: persistence_mae}
```

The last held-out prediction is decoded through `wotex_nx` as an inert
`Prediction`, exactly like the persistence baseline in the window lane.

```elixir
thing_id = "urn:wotex:lab:room:simulated"
{:ok, data_schema} = DataSchema.new(%{"type" => "number", "unit" => "Cel"})

{:ok, prediction_output} =
  OutputSchema.new(
    kind: :prediction,
    thing_id: thing_id,
    affordance_type: :property,
    affordance_name: "temperature",
    data_schema: data_schema,
    dtype: :f32,
    metadata: %{"model" => "ar1-nx", "baseline" => "persistence"}
  )

last_at = List.last(times)
last_prediction = predict.(Nx.tensor([List.last(values)], type: :f32))[0]

{:ok, prediction} =
  Decoder.decode(last_prediction, prediction_output,
    id: "ar1-#{last_at}",
    produced_at: last_at,
    target_at: last_at + simulation.manifest["step"]
  )

prediction
```

The experiment manifest records digests of the dataset and the model
parameters, the split boundary, the normalization statistics and the cohort.

```elixir
{:ok, dataset_json} = Wotex.JSON.encode(%{"times" => times, "values" => values})
{:ok, parameters_json} = Wotex.JSON.encode(%{"coefficient" => Nx.to_number(coefficient)})

manifest =
  Map.merge(simulation.manifest, %{
    "dataset_digest" => Digest.bytes(dataset_json),
    "model" => "ar1-nx",
    "parameters_digest" => Digest.bytes(parameters_json),
    "split" => %{"train_rows" => split_at, "test_rows" => length(values) - split_at, "boundary" => boundary},
    "normalization" => %{"mean" => Nx.to_number(mean), "std" => Nx.to_number(std), "from" => "train"},
    "window" => %{"width" => 2, "built_after_split" => true},
    "metrics" => %{"model_mae" => model_mae, "persistence_mae" => persistence_mae},
    "dtype" => "f32",
    "backend" => inspect(Nx.default_backend()),
    "compiler" => "Nx.Defn.Evaluator",
    "toolchain" => Digest.toolchain(Nx.BinaryBackend)
  })

Map.take(manifest, ["dataset_digest", "parameters_digest", "split", "metrics"])
```

## Deliberate breakage

Reading held-out statistics into normalization is the classic leak. The
guard below is the check a consumer must keep: statistics derived from the
full stream differ from the training statistics, and the manifest names
which one was used.

```elixir
leaky_mean = Nx.to_number(Nx.mean(Nx.tensor(values, type: :f32)))
leak_detected? = leaky_mean != Nx.to_number(mean)
{leaky_mean, Nx.to_number(mean), leak_detected?}
```

The actual bounded Axon lane records values, masks and quality in its six-field
window input, trains after the split, decodes an inert prediction, and returns
a serialized parameter artifact. Experiment contract `2.0.0` computes its future
prediction from the latest two observed rows, separately from held-out scoring.
Its manifest records those input times. Parameters use public `Nx.serialize/2`
so native buffers do not escape as unusable resource references; old raw-term
artifacts must be regenerated. The same small profile also runs on EXLA CPU;
the comparison tolerance permits backend rounding but identity fields remain
exact.

```elixir
{:ok, axon_binary} = RoomModel.run(count: 32, split_at: 24, epochs: 2)

{:ok, axon_exla} =
  RoomModel.run(
    backend: EXLA.Backend,
    compiler: EXLA,
    count: 32,
    split_at: 24,
    epochs: 2,
    timeout_ms: 30_000
  )

binary_prediction = Prediction.to_map(axon_binary.prediction)
exla_prediction = Prediction.to_map(axon_exla.prediction)
backend_delta = abs(binary_prediction.value - exla_prediction.value)

# These are trusted artifacts just produced above, not uploaded parameter bytes.
restored_binary = Nx.with_default_backend(Nx.BinaryBackend, fn -> Nx.deserialize(axon_binary.parameters, [:safe]) end)
restored_exla = Nx.with_default_backend(Nx.BinaryBackend, fn -> Nx.deserialize(axon_exla.parameters, [:safe]) end)
```

## Safe telemetry

No Lab span wraps the consumer's own Nx arithmetic; the lane's spans arrive
with the encoder and decoder calls and follow the same allowlist as every
other notebook. The manifest itself contains digests and numbers only: no
paths, no callbacks, no credentials.

```elixir
manifest_public? =
  not (inspect(manifest) =~ "/Users") and not (inspect(manifest) =~ "/home") and
    Enum.all?(Map.values(manifest), &(not is_function(&1))) and
    not (inspect(axon_binary.manifest) =~ "/Users") and
    not (inspect(axon_binary.manifest) =~ "/home")
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `split-before-window` | The split boundary is the 49th sample time and windows are built afterwards |
| `no-window-leak` | No training window reaches the boundary and no held-out window precedes it |
| `train-statistics-only` | The training mean differs from the held-out mean and is the one used |
| `model-vs-persistence-scored` | Both scores exist on the same held-out rows and the decoded prediction targets the next step |
| `manifest-digest` | Dataset and parameter digests are `sha256:` values |
| `axon-training` | An Axon parameter artifact and inert decoded prediction are returned |
| `backend-cohort` | Binary/Evaluator and EXLA CPU agree within the declared tolerance |

```elixir
%{
  "split-before-window" => boundary == 48_000 and manifest["window"]["built_after_split"],
  "no-window-leak" => no_leak?,
  "train-statistics-only" => leak_detected? and manifest["normalization"]["from"] == "train",
  "model-vs-persistence-scored" =>
    is_float(model_mae) and is_float(persistence_mae) and
      match?(%Prediction{target_at: target} when target == last_at + 1_000, prediction),
  "manifest-digest" =>
    String.starts_with?(manifest["dataset_digest"], "sha256:") and
      String.starts_with?(manifest["parameters_digest"], "sha256:") and manifest_public?,
  "axon-training" =>
    axon_binary.manifest["model"] == "axon-room-v1" and byte_size(axon_binary.parameters) > 0 and
      axon_binary.manifest["experiment_version"] == "2.0.0" and
      match?(%Axon.ModelState{}, restored_binary) and match?(%Axon.ModelState{}, restored_exla) and
      match?(%Prediction{}, axon_binary.prediction),
  "backend-cohort" =>
    backend_delta < 0.0001 and axon_exla.manifest["backend"] == "EXLA.Backend" and
      axon_binary.manifest["feature_order"] == axon_exla.manifest["feature_order"]
}
```

## Public seam

The consumer contract is the observation and decoded-output shape of
`wotex_nx:WNX.01`; Axon and EXLA are optional execution profiles, not WoT
semantics owners.

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.03 `axon-room-model` and `backend-cohort` lanes, implementation
  status implemented (`WLB-C03`, `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream consumer evidence: `WNX-C04`, `WNX-C05`.

## Replacement adapter

Replace the small Axon model with your own explicit model function; keep the
split, training-only statistics, limits and manifest fields. Any model output
must be decoded through `Wotex.Nx.Decoder.decode/3` before a policy sees it.
